    <b>Now — v0.3.2 "dynamic configuration" (formerly v0.3.0): be deployable the way Envoy is deployed.</b> Current
    since v0.3.1 shipped. Everything below about this gate was written while it was labelled
    v0.3.0. <b>WP-20 is DONE, 2026-09-05/06 — all six
    increments (0 through 5).</b> Increment 5 landed all five WP-28 acceptance scenarios {ACK,
    NACK-recover, reconnect-resync, spurious-nonce, out-of-order-response} over a real socket, plus
    <code>make xds-conformance</code>, which <b>found a real deadlock on its first run</b>
    against a pinned go-control-plane: our client queued its initial request only after awaiting
    stream headers, and grpc-go withholds those until it has a request to answer — our own puppet
    harness structurally cannot catch this, since tonic sends headers eagerly. The client is also
    now reachable from the shipped binary (increment 4d-iii) — until that landed, every piece of
    WP-20 was built, tested, and unreachable. RDS and EDS moved to WP-21, which now subscribes,
    applies, and warms their dependent resources. One ordering gap stays open,
    deliberately and in writing: ACK-before-apply in <code>main.rs</code>, which two independent
    test attempts could not observe from outside (the ACK's own network round trip dwarfs the
    race window) — publish-ordering is closed instead by the type system.
    <b>WP-21 (warming, ordering, make-before-break) started 2026-09-06, and increment 2 landed
    EDS ingest end to end on 2026-09-07.</b> An ADS-delivered <code>ClusterLoadAssignment</code>
    now lowers through the same converter the static path uses, resolves to the cluster(s)
    subscribing to it, and reaches <code>ClusterManager::accept_eds_update</code> — proven by an
    <code>ads_e2e</code> scenario that routes a real request through the result. Two bugs were
    caught in review rather than by a test, and both are the same shape: a constraint written
    down in one increment that the next increment did not honour. Increment 2a accepted a
    hostname in an EDS endpoint (nothing resolves one — refused at lowering now), and 2b-ii
    called the sink with the EDS <i>resource</i> name where <code>accept_eds_update</code> is
    keyed by <code>Cluster.name</code>, which Envoy lets differ. <code>/ready</code> now gates on
    cluster warming, and WP-58 request admission distinguishes <code>cluster_not_warmed</code>
    from <code>no_healthy_upstream</code>. RDS delivery, replacement-listener warming, last-good
    retention, and ADS initial-fetch timeout landed 2026-09-11. Init-manager sequencing and full
    5k-rps, Envoy-baselined rename churn remain ahead of WP-21.
    <b>WP-54 (tenant self-service usage API) is DONE, 2026-09-06, both increments</b> — not
    gate-moving, it never was part of the ADS gate. <b>WP-55 (usage history) was decided
    2026-09-06 — export-only, stay stateless — and DONE 2026-09-07</b>: a <code>tenant</code>
    dimension on the token-usage instrument, capped at 4000 series so an unbounded tenant space
    folds into one overflow series rather than growing the registry, dropping the dimension and
    never the measurement. It was resized L → S once its own stale starting claim (the
    token-usage metric "never recorded") was checked against the code and found wrong.
    <b>WP-21 increment 3 landed 2026-09-07: <code>/ready</code> now gates on cluster
    warming</b>, which finally gives <code>ClusterManager::is_initialized</code> a production
    caller — it had none outside its own unit tests since WP-16, so warming was inert for every
    discovery type. The deadline (<code>initial_fetch_timeout</code>) never marks a cluster
    initialized: it only stops warming gating readiness, so WP-58 can still tell "never fed"
    from "all hosts ejected". <b>WP-56 landed the same day</b> — endpoint metadata lowers into
    the IR, with <code>envoy.lb</code> refused by dotted path rather than accepted and ignored,
    because accepting a subset-routing selector we do not implement would leave an operator's
    exclusion list installed and never enforced. <b>WP-59 increment 1 landed too</b>, and it
    closed a live defect: <code>update_from_snapshot</code> published every rebuilt cluster with
    an EMPTY host set and only then resolved them, serially — so a slow STRICT_DNS cluster left
    every other cluster in the same reload empty and rejecting traffic for the whole span.
    <code>swap_under_load.rs</code>, which closed v0.2.0's zero-dropped-streams clause, uses
    STATIC clusters only and structurally could not see it.
    <b>WP-60 and WP-61 shipped 2026-09-07</b> (composable spend counters — the absolute-write
    design loses spend with two writers rather than double-counting it, the unsafe direction —
    and policy propagation by version-stamp polling, bound decided at 60s), which unblocked
    <b>WP-62 (Redis-backed shared stores): increments 1-3 landed 2026-09-07</b> — reconciliation
    staleness is observable, a Redis-backed store (Lua scripts for the token check, 13 tests
    green against real Redis 7), and the store is now selectable from config. <b>Not proven yet,
